<exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="exercisegroup-vector-geom-distance">
<title>Computing distance</title>

<introduction><p>
	Compute the distance between points <m> P </m> and <m> Q </m> by computing the norm of an appropriate vector.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> P(3,5) </m>,
		<m> Q(7,4) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		In <xref ref="exercise-vectors-geometric-vecs-1st-to-1st" />,
		we calculated the vector corresponding to the directed line segment <m>\abray{PQ}</m> as
		<m> \uvec{v} = (4,-1) </m>.
		The distance between <m> P </m> and <m> Q </m> is the norm of this vector:
		<md> d(P,Q) = \unorm{v} = \sqrt{17} </md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> P(2,-1) </m>,
		<m> Q(-1,2) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		In <xref ref="exercise-vectors-geometric-vecs-4th-to-2nd" />,
		we calculated the vector corresponding to the directed line segment <m>\abray{PQ}</m> as
		<m> \uvec{v} = (-3,3) </m>.
		The distance between <m> P </m> and <m> Q </m> is the norm of this vector:
		<md> d(P,Q) = \unorm{v} = 3 \sqrt{2} </md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> P( 3,6) </m>,
		<m> Q(-3,3) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		In <xref ref="exercise-vectors-geometric-vecs-1st-to-2nd" />,
		we calculated the vector corresponding to the directed line segment <m>\abray{PQ}</m> as
		<m> \uvec{v} = (-6,-3) </m>.
		The distance between <m> P </m> and <m> Q </m> is the norm of this vector:
		<md> d(P,Q) = \unorm{v} = 3 \sqrt{5} </md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> P\left( - \dfrac{6}{5}, - \dfrac{ 2}{9} \right) </m>,
		<m> Q\left(   \dfrac{3}{5},   \dfrac{11}{9} \right) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		In <xref ref="exercise-vectors-geometric-vecs-with-fracs" />,
		we calculated the vector corresponding to the directed line segment <m>\abray{PQ}</m> as
		<m> \uvec{v} = (9/5, 13/9) </m>.
		The distance between <m> P </m> and <m> Q </m> is the norm of this vector:
		<md> d(P,Q) = \unorm{v} = \frac{\sqrt{10786}}{45} </md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> P(-3,-1,-1) </m>,
		<m> Q( 9,-1,-1) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		The vector corresponding to the directed line segment <m>\abray{PQ}</m> is
		<m> \uvec{v} = (12,0,0) </m>.
		The distance between <m> P </m> and <m> Q </m> is the norm of this vector:
		<md> d(P,Q) = \unorm{v} = 12 </md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> P(-2, 5,8) </m>,
		<m> Q( 8,-6,1) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		The vector corresponding to the directed line segment <m>\abray{PQ}</m> is
		<m> \uvec{v} = (10,-11,-7) </m>.
		The distance between <m> P </m> and <m> Q </m> is the norm of this vector:
		<md> d(P,Q) = \unorm{v} = 3 \sqrt{30} </md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> P(2,-8,8,-3) </m>,
		<m> Q(3,-8,4,-6) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		The vector corresponding to the directed line segment <m>\abray{PQ}</m> is
		<m> \uvec{v} = (1,0,-4,-3) </m>.
		The distance between <m> P </m> and <m> Q </m> is the norm of this vector:
		<md> d(P,Q) = \unorm{v} = \sqrt{26} </md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> P(-1,0,2,4, 1) </m>,
		<m> Q(-1,3,4,5,-5) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		The vector corresponding to the directed line segment <m>\abray{PQ}</m> is
		<m> \uvec{v} = (0,3,2,1,-6) </m>.
		The distance between <m> P </m> and <m> Q </m> is the norm of this vector:
		<md> d(P,Q) = \unorm{v} = 5 \sqrt{2} </md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
